text:
support-anna: customer report — using our TS sdk v0.12.2 on Node 18.16, undici 5.8. They see repeated charges when requests time out and the client retries. Billing alerted. stack: Fetch -> SDK retry middleware -> AbortController reused across attempts.
support-anna: here's the failing snippet they sent:
```ts
const ctl = new AbortController();
await sdk.client.post('/v1/generate', { body, signal: ctl.signal });
// on error we call retry which re-uses ctl and calls ctl.abort() between attempts
```
support-anna: customer says sometimes the second attempt still goes through server-side while the client thinks it aborted, so they got charged twice.
alex: ugh. can you paste request ids / headers? also node + undici versions?
support-anna: request id: req_01GZX... , x-retry-count: 1 in second request in our logs, user environment undici@5.8.0, node v18.16.0, ts-sdk 0.12.2.
maria: quick note: AbortSignal is single-use — once aborted it stays aborted. If the SDK is re-using the same signal across retry attempts that's a bug. undici respects the AbortSignal strongly and won't re-attach after aborted.
raj: also undici had an internal change around 5.7 -> 5.8 where signal handling tightened up. Re-using controllers could lead to odd behavior: sometimes the runtime emits fetch to server before abort propagates and you end up with a partially-sent request that still completes server-side.
sam: can we repro locally? minimal repro script pls.
maria: here's a repro I used quickly (node + undici):
```js
// repro.js
const { fetch } = require('undici');
let ctl = new AbortController();
async function attempt() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/delay?ms=500', { signal: ctl.signal, method: 'POST' });
    console.log('ok', await res.text());
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('err', e.name);
  }
}
(async () => {
  // simulate retry middleware that re-uses controller
  await attempt();
  // reuse same controller for next attempt
  ctl = ctl; // re-use -> still aborted if aborted before
  await attempt();
})();
```

maria: if you instead create a fresh controller per attempt, undici behaves as expected. same with fetch global in node16+ polyfills.
raj: fix options: (1) change SDK retry middleware to create a new AbortController for each attempt and pipe timeout into it, (2) make sure user-provided signal is not mutated/reused (clone semantics), (3) document undici/node version compatibility.

support-anna: customer env also had a custom wrapper that passed in their own signal object to SDK and expected SDK to reuse it. we should call that out.
alex: can we add a defensive check: if signal is aborted, don't reuse; or create a new combined controller that respects original but is new per attempt. something like:
```ts
function perAttemptSignal(userSignal) {
  if (!userSignal) return new AbortController().signal;
  if (userSignal.aborted) return userSignal; // let caller know it's already aborted
  const attemptCtl = new AbortController();
  // forward original abort to attemptCtl
  const onAbort = () => attemptCtl.abort();
  userSignal.addEventListener('abort', onAbort, { once: true });
  return attemptCtl.signal;
}
```

raj: yep that pattern works. also ensure we remove listener after attempt to avoid leaks.
sam: created patch branch fix/retry-signal-per-attempt, will add tests plus an integration test against undici 5.6, 5.7, 5.8 to verify behavior.
support-bot: created ticket SPT-3421 linking customer report and engineering patch.
billing-alex: putting billing hold on customer's account until we confirm root cause + mitigation. please add reproduction steps + timeline.
support-anna: added repro and sequence, included minimal scripts, logs, and request ids in the ticket.
maria: small note to docs: call out that AbortController signals are not reusable across retry attempts, recommend SDK consumers either pass no signal (let SDK manage timeouts) or pass a signal that they expect to be global.
alex: for release plan — ship patch v0.12.3 that ensures per-attempt controller. also add note in changelog about undici compatibility.
sam: rolling patch to beta channel now, will notify when available.
sam: quick heads-up: we also found a thin race where backoff timer + abort ordering could still produce duplicate server-side completion for idempotent POSTs. recommend customers use idempotency-key header for sensitive ops as additional protection.
support-anna: forwarded that to customer. they appreciate the quick turnaround.
sam: (after patch) patched and released 0.12.3-beta. test customer confirmed no duplicates in their test harness.
raj: follow-up action items: 1) add unit test for signal reuse, 2) update SDK docs, 3) add integration test matrix for undici versions, 4) create migration note.
alex: thanks everyone :thumbsup:
support-anna: marking this thread resolved for now.
